[... byte cap hit — full stream in out/run-logs/ ...]
ne, so re-splitting to satisfy the bound would restore the duplication the consolidation removed.\", 'acceptance_criteria': \"A ready adjudication row admits ahead of every other class, the ruled rank table unchanged (--explain still reports it) and non-adjudication rows keeping their relative order; an unresolved handback is selected before spine or implementation work, and processing it either resolves the record or mints a provenance-linked successor exactly once or names an already-open work item that supersedes the closed work item as its successor; with no unresolved handback, only the earliest incomplete spine tier is eligible; implementation work is ineligible while any required test case remains unlaid; at human_approval_through = 0 no spine tier produces a human hold, each higher level holds exactly its documented cumulative tiers, and a human-held attestation still drains and surfaces with the adjudication row waiting behind the stop; every selection records the chosen class, source identifier and hold decision. Two resumes over the same tracked state select the same eligible set in the same order; an item with missing or contradictory declared inputs is excluded with a stated reason while disjoint classified work stays eligible; no prose surface participates in the derivation; the ordering and exclusion reasons are exposed for replay. A fresh scaffold ships no next-wi/run-phase pointer and is parallel-by-default; no live instruction or executable surface reads, writes, validates, generates or links such a pointer; a build-out lane's phase resolves from its branch name with no pointer file; an upgraded or resynced repository that once carried such a pointer carries neither pointer file after the migration and no live dependency on either survives, so the deletion holds for a migrated repository and not only for a fresh scaffold; the status snapshot regenerates mechanically and its freshness is gated.\", 'priority': 'S', 'verification': 'Test', 'status': 'Approved', 'phase': 5, 'da_refs': ['DA-006'], 'aspect': 'unattended-loop'}\r\nSR-165 {'title': 'The component partition carries a reproducible derivation record', 'sn_refs': ['SN-040'], 'boundary_refs': ['B-05'], 'hat_refs': ['MAINTAINER'], 'requirement': \"The delivered planning content shall leave, with every component partition it produces, a machine-readable derivation record in the tracked component registry rather than session prose — the candidate partitions considered, the declared objective and constraints they were scored against, each candidate's score, the selected partition, and the human ruling that named the components and adopted it — such that a live component row whose record omits any of those elements, or a selection the recorded scores do not rank first without a recorded human override, is a reportable finding.\", 'rationale': \"Realizes SN-040 — a free-form partition leaves no record of the alternatives weighed, so the next reviser cannot tell load-bearing boundaries from accidents; the record is what lets the choice be re-examined. SN-040 had no citing SR, even as the P5 partition work performed exactly this record. Reproducibility is stated as a recomputation from the record's own declared inputs, not as a reviewer re-running a model session, because only the former can fail. B-05 alone: the human ruling is an element the record must carry, not an observable the system states at the authority input, which is SR-139's and SR-140's ground.\", 'acceptance_criteria': \"A partition round writes the derivation record into the tracked component registry, never into session prose or a plan document alone; a live component row with no record, or a record omitting the candidate partitions, the declared objective, the constraints, any candidate's score, the selected partition or the human ruling, is reported naming the missing element; a selected partition the recorded scores do not rank first is reported unless the record carries the human override that chose it; the recorded ranking recomputes from the record's own objective, constraints and scores alone.\", 'priority': 'S', 'verification': 'Test', 'status': 'Approved', 'phase': 5, 'coincident': 'The need asks for a repeatable explanation of the component partition; the recorded candidates, objective, scores, selection and human ruling are that explanation, and a record missing any of them is reported.', 'aspect': 'process'}\r\nSR-178 {'title': 'Text that has moved away from its acceptance record is reported', 'sn_refs': ['SN-029'], 'boundary_refs': ['B-02'], 'hat_refs': ['TEST-ENGINEER'], 'requirement': 'The kit shall report any recorded artifact whose text has moved away from the\\ncopy recording its acceptance - stakeholder needs included - regardless of any\\nStatus movement.', 'rationale': 'THE DRIFT half, separable from SR-140 in the direction that matters: a record that exists and rides its approval commit, with nothing ever comparing live text against it, satisfies SR-140 completely and leaves every later amendment invisible. That is not a hypothetical - it is precisely the failure the git-history derivation had, and the reason a baseline on disk was owed at all.\\nREGARDLESS OF STATUS MOVEMENT is the load-bearing clause, not a qualifier. The amendment machinery watches the Status flip, and the sanctioned staged-amendment path deliberately does not flip - so a rule keyed off the flip returns nothing on exactly the rows a sitting exists to judge. Reporting against the recorded copy is the only reading that survives that, because the copy is a baseline OUTSIDE the live file and a walk over the live file can never be one.\\nTHE STAKEHOLDER-NEED RIDER is in the normative text rather than left to the acceptance because the need tier is the one a sitting most wants to hold and the one the amendment-watching rules were last to reach: each rule walks the tiers it is given, so without the clause the need tier is left off the walk and no rule reaches it. Needs are compared row by row under the same approved and traced split as every other tier.\\nWHAT COUNTS AS MOVEMENT is one definition with one home, shared with the amend-without-flip guard: the id column and the Status cell are excluded STRUCTURALLY (the id is the join key rather than content, and folding Status in would make every flip read as an amendment and every amendment invisible behind its own flip), and the remainder splits into an approved half and a traced half. Two readers of one basis rather than two rules that agree until they do not.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'An artifact whose normative text has moved since the copy that recorded its acceptance is reported whether or not its Status moved; an approved cell moving under a row still claiming approval is reported, a merely traced cell moving is not, and a row below approval has no claim to fall from and is never reported; the status marker itself is never read as the movement, so a flip is neither an amendment nor a mask for one; and stakeholder needs, assumptions and surrogates are held to the same rule.', 'priority': 'S', 'verification': 'Test', 'status': 'Approved', 'phase': 5, 'da_refs': ['DA-003'], 'aspect': 'process'}\r\nTC-271 {'verifies': ['SR-178', 'LLR-273', 'IF-126'], 'level': 'Integration', 'method': \"Driven on real git repositories. A record seeded at one commit whose test-case copy alone is re-written at a later one, the directory-wide stamp naming the later commit: the re-attestation brief from the command line names the requirement and design registries' copies with the seeding commit and the test-case registry's with the later one, the open-items view lists the same pairs, and the model's entry carries each spine registry's own stamp; the first-approval brief's anchor names the design registry it copies with the seeding commit alone.\", 'tier': 'Full', 'expected': \"Satisfies LLR-273 (parent SR-178): each owner and judge surface names the commit that last wrote each registry's own copy.\", 'automated': 'Yes', 'evidence': 'tests/test_snapshot_readers.py::test_the_owners_brief_and_open_items_name_each_registrys_own_copy; tests/test_adjudicate_brief.py::test_the_first_approval_anchor_names_the_copied_registrys_own_copy', 'status': 'Approved', 'phase': 6}\r\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_19","type":"command_execution","command":"\"C:\\\\Program Files\\\\PowerShell\\\\7\\\\pwsh.exe\" -Command '$env:PYTHONIOENCODING='\"'utf-8'; @'\nfrom pathlib import Path\np=Path('C:/Projects/ai-template.wt/review-tmp/2026-10-26a-leg23/p11/TC-211-rejudge.md')\nreport='''Inspected: 28a03090936ffb4276d47c4accfc2f0ff119a38b\n\n# TC-211 independent re-judgement\n\nInspector: fresh Codex session, 2026-10-26. I authored none of the inspected requirements, briefs or verdicts. This is an Inspection, not a rerun of the unit/integration evidence. Repository commands were read-only; only this report was written.\n\nBasis: the whole TC-211 row, \"'`docs/test/test-cases.toml:2174–2188`; procedure `docs/test/inspection-procedures.md:51–63`, abnormal inputs at lines 178–185; rubric `docs/rubrics/decomposition-proportionality.md`, revision last changed at b334107660a436c21b189397f42cb2bcad150532 (read at the inspected HEAD). All R anchors must pass and no B pattern may occur.\n\nSample:\n\n- `docs/reviews/wi-976/016-ADJUDICATE-9d6ad4c.brief.md`\n- `docs/reviews/wi-976/016-ADJUDICATE-9d6ad4c.md`\n\nOther sitting verdicts read as chain evidence (each path below is also the citation shorthand for its numbered sitting):\n\n- `docs/reviews/wi-976/004-ADJUDICATE-81e623b.md`\n- `docs/reviews/wi-976/010-ADJUDICATE-5eac2e5.md`\n- `docs/reviews/wi-976/011-ADJUDICATE-a46aad8.md`\n- `docs/reviews/wi-976/012-ADJUDICATE-066e66c.md`\n- `docs/reviews/wi-976/015-ADJUDICATE-91ed238.md`\n\n## 1. Independently resolved applicability — satisfied\n\nSN-036 has no tags (`docs/requirements/stakeholder-needs.toml:302–308`). Its prose “Scope: template” does not declare a scope context. The roster says undeclared fields satisfy no predicate and that applicability uses need tags plus work-item Workstream and SafetyClass (`docs/requirements/hats.toml`, grammar commentary preceding the roster). WI-976'\"'s frontmatter declares \"'`workstream = \"process\"` and `safety_class = \"ordinary\"` (`docs/work/active/wi-976/WI-976-retire-perspective-sidecar.md:1–13`). The widened tag set is therefore {process, ordinary}.\n\nTen hats apply by `always`: SECURITY (roster line 71), MAINTAINER (99), TEST-ENGINEER (105), UX-DESIGNER (111), UX-ENGINEER (117), ACCESSIBILITY (138), PERFORMANCE (154), CONSISTENCY (174), INTEGRITY-RECOVERABILITY (202), PRODUCT-FITNESS (214). FIRST-RUN-ADOPTER applies because its disjunction includes `tags contains \"process\"` (81).\n\nUNATTENDED-OPS (87), CROSS-PLATFORM (93), LEGAL (123) and DATA-PROTECTION (128) do not apply: none of their qualifying tags is present. I evaluated these predicates independently of the sample'\"'s list. Result: eleven applicable hats.\n\n## 2. Brief questions and verdict outcomes — satisfied (R1; no B1)\n\nThe sample brief presents every applicable question verbatim at lines 683–714, in the order below. Its lines 716–722 require one outcome per hat for the whole sitting. The actual verdict has exactly one outcome per applicable hat:\n\n| Hat | Brief question line | Verdict outcome line |\n|---|---:|---:|\n| SECURITY | 683 | 34 |\n| FIRST-RUN-ADOPTER | 686 | 35 |\n| MAINTAINER | 689 | 36 |\n| TEST-ENGINEER | 692 | 37 |\n| UX-DESIGNER | 695 | 38 |\n| UX-ENGINEER | 698 | 39 |\n| ACCESSIBILITY | 701 | 40 |\n| PERFORMANCE | 704 | 41 |\n| CONSISTENCY | 707 | 42 |\n| INTEGRITY-RECOVERABILITY | 710 | 43 |\n| PRODUCT-FITNESS | 713 | 44 |\n\nMAINTAINER and TEST-ENGINEER name raised rows and Hat-Refs; the other outcomes give no-finding reasons. CONSISTENCY explicitly preserves the successor obligation. These are outcomes in the actual review record, not a substitute sidecar or an inference from row provenance.\n\n## 3. Complete chain and returned rows — satisfied within the ruled scope\n\nThe live SN-036 → SR-161 chain retains LLR-183, LLR-202 and LLR-360, with TC-178, TC-198, TC-399 and direct Inspection TC-211. SR-186 also links directly to TC-211. I read those rows, including the unchanged LLR-202/TC-198 included in the sample brief's complete chain. The removed LLR-297/TC-312 are explicitly judged as removals rather than silently omitted.\n\n- Sitting 011 returned TC-178's false unused-layer coverage advisory claim (lines 32–37, 74–88). Sitting 012 re-sat and blessed its corrected Method and narrowed Expected (lines 16–20). Sitting 016 carries that blessing at line 21.\n- Sitting 012's act could not absorb the unjudged retirements. Sitting 015 records this at lines 5–16 and explicitly blesses LLR-297 and TC-312 as removals at lines 29–30; sitting 016 repeats those blessings at lines 14–15.\n- Sitting 015 returned LLR-273's new module qualifier (lines 21–24, 37, 67–81). Sitting 016 re-sat and blessed the exact remedy, retaining the approved symbol and dropping its module qualifier (line 18).\n- Sitting 016 blesses LLR-167, TC-161, SR-161, LLR-183, TC-178 and TC-211, rules SR-165 CLARITY, and approves LLR-360/TC-399 (lines 16–23, 29–32). \"'`docs/archive/last_approved/acts.toml:970–980` records act 160'\"'s re-attestations, including the two removals, and act 161's approvals. Approval status alone was not used as closure evidence.\n- Sitting 004's route-dependent tag widening defect is a SUCCESSOR, not a returned row (lines 27–38). Sitting 010 confirms it unchanged and unaffected on this sample, then dismisses the duplicate finding (lines 7–12, 21–31). It remains owed; this inspection does not assert general route conformance.\n\nSN-036 is a held-rung MEANING recommendation in sitting 016:13, not a completed re-attestation. I read D-023 itself (\"'`docs/decisions/wi-976.toml:163–168`): the owner expressly moved that act to trunk after landing because the held-rung lane rule refuses this MEANING act. Acts 160/161 do not include SN-036. The pending owner act is accurately bounded and authorized by D-023; I do not misread its existing Approved status as evidence of a fresh act or treat the explicitly deferred act as an unanswered row return.\n\n## 4. Independent purposes and stopping decision — partly satisfied; R2 fails\n\nThe retained children have distinct purposes within the required spine:\n\n| Child / verification | Independent purpose and evidence |\n|---|---|\n| SR-161 | Requires the brief'\"'s applicable questions and the verdict's actual outcomes, realizing SN-036 (\"'`system-requirements.toml:806–818`). |\n| LLR-183 / TC-178 | Row provenance, name resolution, severity and derived effective sets; separate from round coverage (`low-level-requirements.toml:1906–1917`; `test-cases.toml:1810–1819`). |\n| LLR-202 / TC-198 | Staged substantive amendments missing Hat-Refs, including silence on traced-only changes and inapplicable tiers; this is a temporal amendment check, distinct from name resolution (`low-level-requirements.toml:2124–2135`; `test-cases.toml:2019–2028`). |\n| LLR-360 / TC-399 | Round detection, parent/tag applicability, optional slots, combined composition, roster absence/refusal and non-ruling outcome syntax (`low-level-requirements.toml:3721–3731`; `test-cases.toml:3947–3956`; sitting 016:31–32). |\n| TC-211 | Human inspection of actual outcomes, retained child purposes and the stopping decision; unit tests that merely compose a question cannot supply this judgement (`test-cases.toml:2174–2188`). |\n| SR-186 | Separately requires independent child value and a recorded stop, while retaining required tiers (`system-requirements.toml:1144–1157`). |\n\nThe ancillary amended branch also has independent purposes: LLR-167/TC-161 ensure complete, scoped evidence/refusal, now including removed rows (`low-level-requirements.toml:1705–1715`; `test-cases.toml:1612–1621`; sitting 015:31–36). LLR-273 names each registry'\"'s own baseline, independently verified by TC-271, rather than another registry's newest copy (\"'`low-level-requirements.toml:2870–2879`; TC-271'\"'s Method/Expected). SR-165's component derivation record is a separate partition obligation; only its obsolete rationale analogy changes (sittin